Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Lanterns Swallow the Low Tide
- Story & Context: On a frozen November run between Holyhead and Crewe, producer Julian Vance plugged his pocket synth into an empty buffet car's PA while ferry workers slept against their duffel bags, capturing the rhythmic rail-squeal on a dictaphone just as the salt-fog rolled in off the docks. The train ground to a sudden halt on the coastal causeway under a flashing amber signal, where Julian tracked the live cello passage right there on the platform bench while singing into his coat collar: "Tell the water we are holding our breath until dawn." That bootleg cassette became the blueprint for the entire harbor-line sound.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic, wide-angle 1990s color film still of a lone diesel train halted on a seaside viaduct at twilight, thick sea mist rolling over the iron rails, warm amber signal lanterns glowing through maritime fog with the words 'Tell the water we are holding our breath' fading into the ocean spray.

Track 2: Slack-Water Turnstile
- Story & Context: Tracked in a draughty signalman's cottage near the Humber estuary, this confessional groove features harbor clerk-turned-vocalist Maren Boyd whispering her late-night shift logs over a gliding 126-BPM progressive bassline and a swell of 432Hz violas. She wrote the lyric on the back of a customs clearance slip after watching late-shift ferry commuters file through the rusty turnstiles without speaking, catching the eye of a boy she hadn’t seen since the rave scene dissolved. Her haunting chorus line, "You pay the toll in silence when the harbor goes cold," blooms right over an opulent symphonic crescendo that resolves into pure sub-bass warmth.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric 1990s editorial photograph of an abandoned, damp harbor turnstile bathed in deep teal maritime shadows, sea foam glistening on rusty iron, a solitary passenger silhouette disappearing into soft glowing mist, evocative of quiet transit and deep emotional release.

Track 3: The Eddystone Recalibration
- Story & Context: Years after the night-train jams ceased, scene archivist David Park recovered an unlabelled reel of 2-inch tape in an abandoned ticket booth at Plymouth Station, finding a 14-minute mythic suite scored for a 16-piece string section and a hypnotic, rolling kick drum. The session notes revealed that the orchestra had tuned their instruments to the resonant acoustic hum of the Eddystone Lighthouse foghorn, creating an otherworldly, restorative drone that commuters swore cured chronic headaches. Over the final, euphoric orchestral descent, a lone train tannoy speaker crackles with the half-remembered transmission: "All vessels drifting inward have arrived at their name."
